1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 Ne4 7. Bxe7 ({"Here} 
7. h4 {does not seem to have been tried in master chess as in Alekhine's
attack in the French Defence. The idea would be the same though, of course,
the position is different. In the French the WN is at e5, on the other hand
Black has not castled." (BCM)}) 7... Qxe7 8. Qc2 c6 9. Bd3 f5 10. O-O Bd7 11.
Ne5 Be8 12. f3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Nd7 14. Nxd7 Qxd7 15. e4 fxe4 16. fxe4 Bg6 17. Qe2
Rxf1+ 18. Rxf1 Rf8 19. Rxf8+ Kxf8 20. Qg4 Qf7 21. a4 dxe4 22. Bxe4 Bxe4 23.
Qxe4 Qf5 24. Qxf5+ exf5 25. Kf2 g5 ({"There was no urgency for this move;} 
25... b6 {as a preventative measure is better." (BCM)}) 26. c5 Ke7 $2 ({
"The losing move. Again,} 26... b6 {was essential and White could at best draw,
e.g.} 27. cxb6 axb6 28. c4 Ke7 29. c5 b5 30. a5 (30. axb5 cxb5 31. d5 b4 32.
Ke3 f4+ 33. Kd4 g4 {and Black wins, as one of the black pawns must queen. If}
34. Ke5 b3 35. d6+ Kd7 36. Kd5 b2 37. c6+ Kd8 38. Ke6 b1=Q 39. c7+ Kc8 40. d7+
Kxc7 41. Ke7 Qe1+ {and wins}) 30... Kd7 31. a6 Kc7 32. Ke3 h5 33. d5 cxd5 34.
Kd4 b4 35. a7 Kb7 36. Kxd5 b3 37. c6+ Kxa7 38. Kd6 b2 39. c7 b1=Q 40. c8=Q {
and Black's extra pawn is neutralised by his king's position."}) 27. a5 {
"The winning move. Black plays the remainder of the game excellently." 
(Probably should be 'White plays..., etc, etc.' - JS)} a6 28. c4 Ke6 29. Kf3 h6
30. Kg3 Kf6 31. d5 Ke5 32. d6 Ke6 33. Kf3 Kf7 34. Ke3 Ke6 35. Kd4 Kf6 36. h3 {
Source: BCM, December 1946, p388} 1-0

